WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.363
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.363 --> 00:00:09.540
In 1990, my mom was having a big year.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.690
She was going up for a huge promotion,
SVP at Morgan Stanley.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:20.510
At the same time, she was going through
a more anxiety inducing process.

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:21.560
She was trying to get pregnant.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:27.300
Now, my mom was the only woman in her
product group and she was a go getter.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.460
She was also 31 and my parents were
having some trouble conceiving.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:36.740
This meant that my mom wasn't
just trying to get pregnant,

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:37.760
she was going through IVF.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:43.974
Her 12 hour work day now featured secret
trips to a broom closet where she

00:00:43.974 --> 00:00:46.997
had to inject a large
needle full of hormones.

00:00:46.997 --> 00:00:50.826
While her male colleagues
went to client happy hours,

00:00:50.826 --> 00:00:55.250
she made up excuses and snuck off or
weekly trips to the doctor.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.571
My mom covered her tracks because she
wasn't sure what her male colleagues would

00:01:00.571 --> 00:01:03.164
think if they found out she
was trying to have a baby.

00:01:03.164 --> 00:01:05.894
As she hid in that closet she wondered,

00:01:05.894 --> 00:01:09.718
what her boss thinks she was
less committed to her job?

00:01:09.718 --> 00:01:13.839
Would should be passed over when
they staff important deals.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:18.520
Ultimately, my mom got that promotion.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:21.040
But along the way, she miscarried.

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:25.970
Now, my mom didn't share any
of this with her coworkers.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:29.027
And it turns out it's probably
good that she didn't.

00:01:29.027 --> 00:01:33.787
Research on bias against pregnant
employees is far ranging and

00:01:33.787 --> 00:01:35.506
scarily conclusive.

00:01:35.506 --> 00:01:38.129
Pregnancy bias is real.

00:01:38.129 --> 00:01:41.633
The workforce is 47% women, and more and

00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:47.609
more of those women are delaying
childbirth to be a part of that workforce.

00:01:47.609 --> 00:01:51.800
But the workplace isn't
changing to meet that reality.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:56.660
Women are still trapped in their
own metaphorical broom closets.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:02.040
It's time to stop the secrecy and
open up a dialogue.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:09.064
So how can employers help reduce stigma
around pregnancy and fertility at work and

00:02:09.064 --> 00:02:13.270
improve outcomes for all women,
all parents in the process?

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:20.090
Before we answer that, first we have
to ask, what is pregnancy bias?

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:22.720
To do that,
we're going to do a little experiment.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:24.405
Raise your hands if you
plan on having children.

00:02:24.405 --> 00:02:28.902
Way more than I practiced
having it's the answer.

00:02:28.902 --> 00:02:33.303
All right, so raise your hands again
if you plan on working while you or

00:02:33.303 --> 00:02:35.030
your partner is pregnant.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:38.310
Same answer, great.

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:39.570
Now, raise your hands again,

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:42.860
if you're going to tell your employer
immediately about your pregnancy.

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:44.890
Nobody.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:46.046
Cool.
All right.

00:02:46.046 --> 00:02:50.387
So men in the room,
you should tell everybody.

00:02:50.387 --> 00:02:55.245
Research shows us that once you're
a parent you'll be paid more,

00:02:55.245 --> 00:02:57.075
hired more frequently.

00:02:57.075 --> 00:02:58.358
Can be seen as more responsible.

00:02:58.358 --> 00:03:04.040
But women, for us the story is different.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:06.630
For us, parenthood is a penalty.

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:14.690
Controlling for hours worked, experience,
industry and education, pregnant women and

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:20.000
new mothers will be hired less,
paid less and seen as less competent.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:24.470
The New York Times found
startling evidence of this,

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:29.270
even relative to their own
evaluations pre-pregnancy.

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:33.420
Once pregnant, women were
immediately seen as less committed.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:37.719
They were left out of important meetings
and not given the same bonuses.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:43.410
And y'all,
this problem is only getting worse.

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:47.070
The Equal Opportunity employment
commission found that this has been rising

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:51.020
for over 20 years when they studied
Pregnancy Discrimination claims.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:53.599
And it's actually at
its highest point ever.

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:58.640
This could be partially caused by
our changing fertility landscape.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:04.530
The average age of first childbirth is
also at its highest ever, especially for

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:06.694
women with undergraduate or
graduate degrees.

00:04:07.885 --> 00:04:10.126
Take us again, as an example.

00:04:10.126 --> 00:04:14.023
The women in this room, will likely
have their first child at age 33.

00:04:14.023 --> 00:04:19.071
But, this trend of later stage
parenthood means that for

00:04:19.071 --> 00:04:22.551
us having a baby might not be as simple.

00:04:22.551 --> 00:04:27.753
Some of us, like my mom,
will need fertility assistance.

00:04:27.753 --> 00:04:32.315
And treatments like IVF are time and
emotion intensive.

00:04:32.315 --> 00:04:37.876
They can impact the work day and compound
our fears of being seen as less committed.

00:04:37.876 --> 00:04:43.196
And though male factor infertility is the
root cause of nearly half of infertility

00:04:43.196 --> 00:04:48.220
cases for couples, the treatments fall
squarely on the shoulders of women.

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:52.400
Now you understand the dynamics in play.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:54.390
The reasons my mom hid in that closet.

00:04:55.470 --> 00:05:00.950
Women are caught in this terrible tension
between pursuing their goals of motherhood

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:05.100
and being seen as less invested to
the careers they spent years building.

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:10.290
And pregnancy bias isn't just bad for
women.

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:13.630
It's bad for families and employers too.

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:16.894
So if you've been chilling
because you don't have a uterus,

00:05:16.894 --> 00:05:18.415
it's time to check back in.

00:05:18.415 --> 00:05:23.670
You need to build this
bias into your budget.

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:26.830
If you plan on having a dual
income household with children,

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:31.280
your female partner will statistically
never recover her earning power.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:35.880
She will make on average $16,000 less
a year after having her first child.

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:41.320
That's a loss of over a million dollars
by the time you're both ready to retire.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:45.960
And as future employers,
we need to prepare for

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:49.350
the attrition of valuable
employees from the workforce.

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:51.650
Given the penalty of motherhood and

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:56.710
the rising cost of childcare many women
debate the economics of returning to work.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:03.430
So it's clear what we must do,
reduce pregnancy bias and

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:07.200
reduce the economic loss of talented
women leaving the workforce.

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:13.880
But hundreds of years of gender bias,
that is a lot to deal with all at once.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:18.639
So let's start by tackling
the stigma around starting a family.

00:06:18.639 --> 00:06:24.675
As base, we can look to for
inspiration is mental health to employers.

00:06:24.675 --> 00:06:29.505
Employees dealing with mental health are
not dissimilar from women trying to get

00:06:29.505 --> 00:06:30.223
pregnant.

00:06:30.223 --> 00:06:34.875
In both cases, people are dealing
with highly emotional considerations.

00:06:34.875 --> 00:06:40.535
Maybe juggling doctor's appointments and
may fear the judgment of their coworkers.

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:45.430
Many companies have successfully
implemented policies to reduce stigma

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:46.780
around mental health.

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:49.340
And specifically,
there are three that we can adopt.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:56.020
First, let's dedicate HR resources
to pregnancy and fertility.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:07:01.210
Let's sponsor educational programs that
clearly lay out fertility policy and

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:01.940
family leave.

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:07.889
Putting our voices strongly behind these
programs demonstrates commitment to

00:07:07.889 --> 00:07:09.931
employees trying to become parents.

00:07:09.931 --> 00:07:13.840
And it creates a culture of support,
not stigma.

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:22.200
Second, let's dedicate physical spaces on
site for women dealing with fertility.

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:26.330
There's no reason that someone going
through IVF should be injecting hormones

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:26.960
in a closet.

00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:31.810
Creating spaces for this,
the same way we do for diabetics,

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:36.430
injecting insulin, helps dispel
the sense of shame and secrecy.

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:40.060
And it also minimizes
interruption to the work day.

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:41.040
It's a win win.

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:47.270
Third and finally,
we need to share stories.

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:51.679
Not just from women but from everyone,
particular from senior leadership.

00:07:51.679 --> 00:07:56.636
Deloitte hosted a series of fireside
chats with partners who were dealing with

00:07:56.636 --> 00:07:58.610
mental health at work.

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:00.570
And the results were immediate.

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:06.496
Employees immediately said that they felt
reduced feelings of stigma and isolation.

00:08:06.496 --> 00:08:12.726
Talking about pregnancy and fertility the
same way not only serves to educate those

00:08:12.726 --> 00:08:18.700
who may not be aware of the impact, but
it makes us more empathetic colleagues.

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:23.410
So sharing stories is
exactly why I'm here today.

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:28.198
Though the first round didn't go
quite as planned, my brother and

00:08:28.198 --> 00:08:31.318
I were the successful results
of IVF cycle number two.

00:08:31.318 --> 00:08:37.482
So I'm here, but
unfortunately my mom isn't.

00:08:37.482 --> 00:08:42.689
We lost her to a battle with cancer
18 years ago, and that's why I'm

00:08:42.689 --> 00:08:48.436
on stage in front of you right now, to
would have wanted me to share her story.

00:08:48.436 --> 00:08:52.450
To keep another woman from
fighting these battles alone.

00:08:53.810 --> 00:08:56.820
So here's my final ask.

00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:59.680
Let's be better future employers.

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:03.992
Let's think about pregnancy bias
when we design our workspaces or

00:09:03.992 --> 00:09:05.844
allocate our HR resources.

00:09:05.844 --> 00:09:11.250
Let's think about pregnancy bias when we
hire, when we staff, when we promote.

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:14.950
Let's share our stories, all of us.

00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:20.760
Let's be more empathetic employers
of not only women, but all parents.

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:25.301
You've listened to me,
now go out and listen to them.

00:09:25.301 --> 00:09:26.899
Thank you.

00:09:26.899 --> 00:09:33.671
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
[MUSIC]

